When money is tight, building an emergency fund and buying health insurance can feel like competing priorities. One keeps cash available when income stops or an unexpected bill arrives. The other protects you from a hospital bill that could otherwise wipe out years of savings.
For someone starting from scratch, the answer is usually not to choose one and ignore the other. SEBI's investor guidance specifically recommends maintaining an emergency fund for unexpected events while having insurance to cover medical emergencies.
The reason health insurance deserves early attention is that an emergency fund has limits. Suppose you have saved Rs. 1 lakh and a hospitalisation costs Rs. 4 lakh. Even a reasonably sized cash reserve may not be enough. A health insurance policy, subject to its terms, can shift a substantial part of an eligible hospitalisation expense away from your savings.
Waiting several years to buy health insurance while building a large emergency fund can also create a problem. Policies may have waiting periods and exclusions, and delaying purchase means you may be older when you first seek cover. The exact waiting periods and exclusions depend on the policy, so these conditions need to be checked before buying.
That does not make an emergency fund less important. Health insurance does not pay for every financial shock. A job loss, sudden home repair, family emergency, temporary loss of income or an expense that falls outside the policy can still require cash.
Consider a salaried person earning Rs. 60,000 a month with essential monthly expenses of Rs. 35,000. If there is no savings buffer, even a two-month interruption in income could create pressure to borrow. Building an emergency reserve alongside insurance gives protection against both medical and non-medical disruptions.
For someone with no health insurance at all, getting adequate health cover should generally not be postponed simply because the emergency fund is still being built. At the same time, the person need not wait until they have accumulated six months of expenses before starting the cash reserve.
The emergency fund can be built gradually. A practical target is often several months of essential expenses, with the exact amount depending on job stability, number of dependants, existing liabilities and access to other financial resources. Someone with
an uncertain income may need a larger buffer than a person with highly stable earnings.
Where should the emergency money be kept? Accessibility matters more than chasing high returns. A combination of a savings account and suitable short-term deposits can be considered, provided the money can be accessed when required. Equity investments are generally unsuitable for the core emergency reserve because their value can fall when the money is urgently needed.
Health insurance needs a separate check. Look beyond the premium and compare the sum insured, room-rent conditions, co-payments, exclusions, waiting periods, network hospitals and claim-related terms. SEBI also advises investors to assess their needs before buying insurance rather than treating every policy as interchangeable.
There is one more situation where the priority becomes clearer. If an employer provides health insurance, check exactly how much it covers and what happens when you leave the job. Employer cover can be useful, but relying on it alone may leave a gap during a career change.
For a household starting with limited savings, a sensible approach can be to secure basic health insurance while simultaneously directing part of each month's surplus towards an emergency fund. Once both foundations are in place, longer-term investments can take a larger share of the budget.
The real danger is postponing either one indefinitely. Insurance protects against a large medical expense; an emergency fund protects against the financial problems that insurance was never designed to cover.
