Nomura expects auto demand to remain strong in September, with broad-based retail growth and recent price hikes having had no material impact on demand. However, growth is expected to taper from 2HFY27F as the industry enters a higher-base period, while rising commodity prices could add to input-cost pressure.
"Dealer surveys suggest strong demand continuing in Sep-26, with broad-based retail growth across segments," Nomura said in its September 28 report. "Recent OEM price hikes have not had any material impact on demand," it said.
The brokerage continues to prefer Mahindra & Mahindra , Hyundai Motor India , Ather Energy , TVS Motor and Sona BLW Precision Forgings , all rated 'Buy'.
Nomura estimates September wholesales to rise 21% year-on-year for passenger vehicles (PVs), 6% for two-wheelers (2Ws) and 25% for medium and heavy commercial vehicles (MHCVs), while tractor volumes are expected to decline 21%.
In PVs, September wholesales are estimated at around 463,000 units, up 21% y-y, while retail sales are expected at 405,000 units, up 26%, adding 58,000 units to inventory. Nomura estimates FY27F/28F PV industry growth at around 13%/6%.
Maruti Suzuki's domestic PV wholesales, excluding OE and LCVs, are estimated at 185,000 units, up 39% y-y. Mahindra & Mahindra's utility vehicle volumes are expected to rise around 12% to around 63,000 units, while Tata Motors' domestic PV sales are estimated to increase around 9% to around 65,000 units. Hyundai Motor India's domestic sales are expected to rise 11% to 57,000 units.
For 2Ws, Nomura estimates September wholesales at 2.313 million units, up 6% y-y, and retail sales at 1.814 million units, up 35%. TVS Motor's overall volumes are expected to rise around 21% y-y, while Bajaj Auto's overall sales are estimated to increase around 12% to 570,000 units. Hero MotoCorp volumes are expected at 715,000 units, up 4%.
MHCV wholesales are estimated at 42,300 units, up 25% y-y, with retail sales at 39,300 units, up 41%. Tractor wholesales are expected to decline 21% due to a shift in festive dates and last year's high base.
Commodity pressure remains elevated as steel, copper and crude prices rise further. This "will further keep input cost pressure elevated and increase the risks for further price hikes by OEMs", Nomura said, adding that CVs and 2Ws are better placed to pass on costs than PVs.
September 2W EV sales are estimated at around 205,500 units, up 87% y-y, with EV penetration "at an inflection point", Nomura said.
