Amid continued selling pressure and a break below crucial support levels, experts advised caution. According to them, the indices could see a bounce after becoming oversold, although the sustainability of any such gains remains uncertain. The Nifty 50 needs to hold support at 22,750, near Monday’s low, for a move towards 23,000. However, a decisive break below this level could drag the index towards 22,500. Meanwhile, if the Bank Nifty fails to defend the 54,400 level, near Monday’s low, further weakness towards 54,000-53,800 cannot be ruled out. On the other hand, a sustained move above 54,400 could push the index towards 55,000.
On September 28, the Nifty 50 plunged 360 points, or 1.56 percent, to 22,780, while the Bank Nifty fell 1,109 points, or 1.99 percent, to 54,472. Market breadth was dominated by bears, with 2,582 shares declining against 694 advancing shares on the NSE.
 Jay Thakkar, Vice President & Head of Derivatives and Quant Research at ICICI Securities 
Even after falling for seven consecutive weeks, the Nifty 50 continued to decline on Monday, indicating that selling pressure remains high. The index broke the critical support level of 23,000 quite swiftly, and there was no pause below that level, with the next support at 22,500, which has the next-highest Put base.
On the upside, until the 23,000 level is reclaimed, there is little scope for a recovery. Hence, the trading range has shifted lower to 22,500-23,000.
FII net index short positions continue to rise and have now crossed 3.10 lakh contracts, indicating an extremely oversold position. However, there has been no reversal in the index so far. The Put-Call Ratio (PCR) is not yet in oversold territory for the current monthly settlement, at 0.62. For the next series as well, it is not significantly oversold. Hence, until the 23,000 level is taken out on the upside, the short-term trend remains sideways to negative.
 Strategy : Sell Nifty Futures with a stop-loss above 23,000 and targets of 22,500 and 22,200.
 Jigar S Patel, Senior Manager - Equity Research at Anand Rathi 
The Nifty has decisively breached the major 22,800 support zone and closed below this level, indicating continued pressure in the near term. However, momentum indicators are showing an interesting divergence. On September 15, when the Nifty registered a lower level, the RSI stood at 22.23, indicating deeply oversold conditions. Despite the recent lower lows in price, the current RSI reading has improved to 27.69. This suggests that while price action remains weak, downside momentum may be losing some intensity.
Additionally, India VIX is trading at elevated levels, reflecting heightened market volatility and risk aversion. Therefore, a sharp recovery or short-covering bounce could emerge over the coming few sessions. Traders should closely monitor the RSI, volatility and price action around the recent lows for confirmation of a potential reversal.
 Strategy : Buy Nifty Futures with a stop-loss at 22,600 and a target of 23,250.
 Somil Mehta, Head of Retail Research at Mirae Asset ShareKhan 
The Nifty has fallen for seven consecutive weeks and is now in its eighth week of decline. There is still no sign of a reversal. The index has closed clearly below the key support zone of 23,000-23,100, indicating that bears remain firmly in control.
If the index bounces, the 23,000-23,050 zone will be the first hurdle. This is the broken support zone, so selling pressure could emerge again around these levels. On the downside, 22,500 is the first level to watch. A break below this level could pull the Nifty down towards the April low of 22,182.
 Strategy : Sell Nifty Futures at CMP or on a bounce towards 23,000, with a stop-loss at 23,200 on a closing basis and targets of 22,500 and 22,182.
 Jay Thakkar, Vice President & Head of Derivatives and Quant Research at ICICI Securities 
The Bank Nifty, too, has been sliding lower and has now closed near the 54,500 level, quite close to the next Put base at 54,000. On the upside, the 56,000 and 57,000 strikes have the highest Call base, making these levels immediate resistance zones. Until these levels are taken out, the short-term trend remains sideways to negative.
The Nifty PSU Bank Index was also quite weak, while the banking sector has attracted significant short positions amid the rise in global bond yields and crude oil prices, which are raising inflationary concerns in the short term.
 Strategy : Sell Bank Nifty Futures on a rise near 55,000, with a stop-loss above 56,000 and a target of 53,000.
 Jigar S Patel, Senior Manager - Equity Research at Anand Rathi 
Bank Nifty is approaching the major support zone of 54,000-54,200. Interestingly, while price action has not breached the June 2026 swing low, the RSI has made a lower low. The current RSI stands at 30.24, compared with 47.57 during the June swing low. This creates a bullish divergence setup, suggesting that downside momentum may be weakening despite continued pressure on price.
A sustained hold above 54,000-54,200, along with an improvement in the RSI, could support a recovery in the coming sessions.
 Strategy : Buy Bank Nifty Futures on dips, with a stop-loss at 53,800 and a target of 55,500.
