Nomura expects a volatile demand and margin environment for consumer companies, but remains positive on select stocks with strong brands, resilient portfolios, pricing power and execution.
Its top picks are Marico , Tata Consumer Products and ITC in consumer staples, and Lenskart, Titan and United Spirits in consumer discretionary - all rated 'Buy'.
The brokerage has a 'Neutral' rating on Hindustan Unilever, Epsilon Advanced Materials and Dabur, while it sees margin pressure for companies including GCPL, Colgate-Palmolive India, Britannia, Nestle India, Tata Consumer Products and paint makers.
"Most raw material prices, except for Brent and HDPE, have either remained range-bound or softened m-m in September. However, they are still up q-q/y-y at levels materially above the product price hikes taken by consumer companies in 1QFY27," Nomura said in its September 28 report.
This could "pressure near-term margins in 2QFY27F, and warrants further product price hikes", it said.
Sugar prices corrected sharply, falling 8% month-on-month and 21% from their peak, but remain up 18% year-on-year, potentially pressuring margins for Britannia, Nestle, Dabur, ITC and HUL.
Copra prices softened 3% month-on-month and remain 32% below last year, which Nomura expects to support Marico's margins. "The recent softness could ease some pressure on margins (from higher packaging cost as well) without necessarily triggering a price cut," it said.
Brent crude and HDPE prices increased 28% and 5% month-on-month and are up 40% and 45% year-on-year, respectively, amid geopolitical uncertainties. Nomura expects this to increase packaging costs for consumer companies and pressure paint margins.
Palm oil prices have remained stable month-on-month but are up 21% year-on-year in rupee terms. The cut in basic customs duty on crude palm oil to 5% from 10%, effective September 24, could provide some relief to GCPL and HUL.
Nomura said GCPL's exposure to crude derivatives and palm oil/PFAD could pressure margins in 2QFY27F, while Colgate could be hit by rising maize and mentha oil prices.
"Gold prices have remained at elevated levels (+46% y-y) but stable during the month (-1% m-m/q-q)," Nomura said, adding that this could support recovery in jewellery footfalls and volume growth.
